+<p>For an original degree pair
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\le j</annotation></semantics></math>,
+retain</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Δ</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\Delta=L_{ij}R_i-R_j,\quad C=\Delta^*O_j\Delta=G_i-G_j,
+\quad R(z)=R_j+z\Delta,
+\quad G(z)=G_j+|z|^2C.
+\tag{7}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(7)</span></div>
+<p>The predecessor constructs the image lattice on
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathbb P^1_{\mathbb C}</annotation></semantics></math>,
+with boundary
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_\infty=\{\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>
+and
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=1/z</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℳ</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>𝒪</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>ker</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\mathcal M_{E_0}=E_0\mathcal O+wE\mathcal O,\qquad E_0=\ker\Delta.
+\tag{8}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(8)</span></div>
+<p>Its smallest A-stable enlargement is
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℳ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mi>𝒪</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝒪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal M_F=F\mathcal O+wE\mathcal O</annotation></semantics></math>,
+where</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+F=E_0+AE_0+\cdots+A^{q-1}E_0.
+\tag{9}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(9)</span></div>
+<p>All statements below concern this original target E. Let
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_F=E/F</annotation></semantics></math>
+denote this finite quotient, not the theta quotient Q.</p>
+<h3 id="the-actual-differential">4.1 The actual differential</h3>
+<p>For any positive integer m, define the lattice</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mi>𝒪</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒪</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\mathcal M_{F,m}=F\mathcal O+\mathcal O(-mD_\infty)E
+\subset E\mathcal O,
+\tag{10}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(10)</span></div>
+<p>and its two-term logarithmic complex, in degrees zero and one,</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><msub><mi>ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><msub><mi>ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>log</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\mathcal K_{F,m}=
+[\mathcal M_{F,m}\xrightarrow{d}
+ \mathcal M_{F,m}\otimes\Omega_X^1(\log D_\infty)].
+\tag{11}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(11)</span></div>
+<p>The differential is the restriction of the constant coefficient
+connection: in the local frame
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^m e</annotation></semantics></math>
+its coefficient is
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\partial_w+m</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Consequently it preserves (10) after tensoring with logarithmic forms.
+Because F is A-stable, A acts on the entire complex. A choice of
+complement
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=F\oplus J</annotation></semantics></math>
+gives the coefficient description
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mi>𝒪</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>𝒪</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>m</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal M_{F,m}=F\mathcal O\oplus J\mathcal O(-m)</annotation></semantics></math>;
+the action can have an off-diagonal block and is not asserted to split
+with that choice.</p>
+<p><strong>Computed cohomology.</strong> The canonical identifications
+as A-modules are</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{\mathbb H^0(X,\mathcal K_{F,m})=F,\qquad
+\mathbb H^1(X,\mathcal K_{F,m})=0,\qquad
+\mathbb H^2(X,\mathcal K_{F,m})=Q_F.}
+\tag{12}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(12)</span></div>
+<p>Here is a direct calculation retaining the coefficients. For the
+compactly supported component, use the two affine charts and the finite
+frame at z. The Cech groups are</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>𝒪</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>span</mo><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+H^1(\mathcal O(-mD_\infty))
+=\operatorname{span}\{z^{-1},\ldots,z^{-(m-1)}\},
+</annotation></semantics></math></div>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>𝒪</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi>Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>log</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>span</mo><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="false">}</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+H^1(\mathcal O(-mD_\infty)\otimes\Omega^1(\log D_\infty))
+=\operatorname{span}\{z^{-1}dz,\ldots,z^{-m}dz\}.
+</annotation></semantics></math></div>
+<p>The induced differential is exactly</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo>↦</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>a</mi><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ z^{-a}\longmapsto-a\,z^{-(a+1)}dz,\qquad 1\le a&lt;m.
+\tag{13}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(13)</span></div>
+<p>It is injective, and its cokernel is the line generated by
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{-1}dz</annotation></semantics></math>.
+For the ordinary logarithmic component the complex is
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>𝒪</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>𝒪</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal O\to\mathcal O(-1)]</annotation></semantics></math>,
+with only the constant H0. The exact sequence of complexes with
+subobject
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>
+and quotient
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_F</annotation></semantics></math>
+then gives (12) canonically, without an A-invariant complement. In
+particular the degree-two identification is residue in the displayed
+coordinate.</p>
+<p>If the original arithmetic cochain degree k is restored by the shift
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>k</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-k]</annotation></semantics></math>,
+the coefficient groups are in degrees k and k+2. For every polynomial
+f,</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mo>Str</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>∣</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>Γ</mi><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>k</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo form="prefix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>Tr</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo>Tr</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mover><mi>A</mi><mo accent="true">‾</mo></mover><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>Tr</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{\operatorname{Str}(f(A)\mid R\Gamma\mathcal K_{F,m}[-k])
+=(-1)^k\bigl(\operatorname{Tr}f(A|F)+\operatorname{Tr}f(\bar A|Q_F)\bigr)
+=(-1)^k\operatorname{Tr}f(A|E).}
+\tag{14}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(14)</span></div>
+<p>The quotient retains all induced Jordan data. This is trace
+additivity on the original exact sequence, not deletion of its second
+term.</p>
+<h3 id="compact-middle-and-ordinary-comparisons">4.2 Compact, middle,
+and ordinary comparisons</h3>
+<p>Put</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mi>!</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>𝒪</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mi>E</mi><msubsup><mi>Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>𝒪</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>E</mi><msubsup><mi>Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>𝒪</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>E</mi><msubsup><mi>Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>log</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\mathcal K_!=[E\mathcal O(-D_\infty)\to E\Omega_X^1],\quad
+\mathcal K_{\mathrm{mid}}=[E\mathcal O\to E\Omega_X^1],\quad
+\mathcal K_*=[E\mathcal O\to E\Omega_X^1(\log D_\infty)].
+\tag{15}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(15)</span></div>
+<p>Their natural inclusions compute the usual compact-support, smooth
+middle, and ordinary comparisons for the constant coefficient system on
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb A^1</annotation></semantics></math>.
+At m=1 the mixed complex fits
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mi>!</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mo>*</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal K_!\to\mathcal K_{F,1}\to\mathcal K_*</annotation></semantics></math>.
+The cohomology groups are</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mi>!</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mi>E</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mi>E</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+H^a(\mathcal K_!)=\begin{cases}E&amp;a=2,\\0&amp;a\ne2,\end{cases}
+\qquad
+H^a(\mathcal K_*)=\begin{cases}E&amp;a=0,\\0&amp;a\ne0,\end{cases}
+\tag{16}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(16)</span></div>
+<p>and
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal K_{\mathrm{mid}}</annotation></semantics></math>
+has E in degrees zero and two. In particular,</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mo>im</mo><mo form="prefix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{\operatorname{im}\bigl(H_c^1(\mathbb A^1,E)\to H^1(\mathbb A^1,E)\bigr)=0.}
+\tag{17}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(17)</span></div>
+<p>This is an application of Deligne’s constant-constituent test to the
+actual constructed curve. It locates its role: it is a metric comparison
+with retained boundary data, not yet the nonconstant cohomological image
+carrying E in degree one. The constructive continuation to such a
+cohomological family begins in Section 5.</p>
+<p>The two boundary comparisons remain explicit. For m=1, the termwise
+quotient
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal K_*/\mathcal K_{F,1}</annotation></semantics></math>
+is the punctual complex
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mover><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Q_F\xrightarrow{0}Q_F]</annotation></semantics></math>
+in degrees zero and one, while
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>𝒦</mi><mi>!</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal K_{F,1}/\mathcal K_!</annotation></semantics></math>
+is
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>F</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mover><mi>F</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[F\xrightarrow{0}F]</annotation></semantics></math>.
+For general m the first quotient has local coefficient differential</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>↦</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ w^a\longmapsto a w^a\,dw/w,\qquad 0\le a&lt;m.
+\tag{18}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(18)</span></div>
+<p>Every positive a is paired with its exact inverse coefficient
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/a</annotation></semantics></math>;
+the constant class survives in both degrees. Thus the extra coherent
+length is kept before its logarithmic differential is taken. In the
+split construction those cycles, boundaries, and their quotients use the
+original fibrewise operations. External absence is never used as the
+image of a represented boundary.</p>
+<h3 id="ramification-and-saturation-retain-the-metric-cost">4.3
+Ramification and saturation retain the metric cost</h3>
+<p>The ramified parameter map
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_m:\mathbb P^1\to\mathbb P^1</annotation></semantics></math>,
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>↦</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\mapsto z^m</annotation></semantics></math>,
+gives the actual representative
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mi>Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_m(z)=R_j+z^m\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Its pulled-back determinant curvature has radial density</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>Tr</mo><mo form="prefix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mi>Z</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo form="postfix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\kappa_m(t)=m^2t^{m-1}\operatorname{Tr}\bigl(Z(I+t^mZ)^{-2}\bigr),
+\qquad Z=G_j^{-1}C.
+\tag{19}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(19)</span></div>
+<p>Substitution
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=t^m</annotation></semantics></math>
+proves</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>∞</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>rank</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{\int_0^\infty\kappa_m(t)dt=m\operatorname{rank}C,\qquad
+\int_0^1\log(1/t)\kappa_m(t)dt=\log(V_i/V_j).}
+\tag{20}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(20)</span></div>
+<p>The pullback on the surviving degree-two de Rham class is
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>m</mi><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dz/z\mapsto m\,dz/z</annotation></semantics></math>.
+It is not an identity disguised by dividing the trace by m.</p>
+<p>In the A-stable enlargement, let
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>dim</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo><mo>dim</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_A=\dim F-\dim E_0</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Its determinant metric in the infinity chart is the old one multiplied
+by
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|w|^{-2ms_A}</annotation></semantics></math>.
+With the retained curvature definition
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>∂</mi><mover><mi>∂</mi><mo accent="true">‾</mo></mover><mo>log</mo><mo>det</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i/2\pi)\partial\bar\partial\log\det G</annotation></semantics></math>,
+the current is</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{c_1((\det\mathcal M_{F,m})^\vee,h^\vee)
+=\mu_m-ms_A\delta_\infty.}
+\tag{21}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(21)</span></div>
+<p>Indeed
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>∂</mi><mover><mi>∂</mi><mo accent="true">‾</mo></mover><mo>log</mo><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i/2\pi)\partial\bar\partial\log|w|^2=\delta_\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Its total mass is
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mo>dim</mo><mi>F</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(q-\dim F)</annotation></semantics></math>,
+the degree of the dual determinant. Full saturation subtracts
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>rank</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\operatorname{rank}C</annotation></semantics></math>
+at infinity and has total degree zero, but leaves the endpoint
+logarithmic integral (20) unchanged. The negative atom is a geometric
+current; it is not a negative support label.</p>
+<h2 id="an-actual-rank-q-cohomological-deformation-of-the-cyclic-packet">5.
+An actual rank-q cohomological deformation of the cyclic packet</h2>
+<p>Write the original monic polynomial in its original coordinate:</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\chi(S)=S^q+\sum_{a=0}^{q-1}c_aS^a.
+\tag{22}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(22)</span></div>
+<p>Introduce two new parameters u and t. They are independent of the
+scalar
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>,
+the arithmetic coordinate S, the logarithmic comparison parameter z, and
+the tensor degree k. Put
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathbb C[u,t]</annotation></semantics></math>
+and define</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\Phi(S)=\frac{S^{q+1}}{q+1}+\sum_{a=0}^{q-1}\frac{c_aS^{a+1}}{a+1},
+\qquad\Phi_t(S)=\Phi(S)-tS.
+\tag{23}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(23)</span></div>
+<p>This is the specified primitive with
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
+with every coefficient
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(a+1)</annotation></semantics></math>
+written. It changes no source coefficient in
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Define the two-term complex</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mover><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mover><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo form="prefix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\mathcal C_{u,t}=[R[S]\xrightarrow{\mathcal D_{u,t}}R[S]dS],
+\qquad
+\mathcal D_{u,t}(P)=\bigl(uP'(S)+(\chi(S)-t)P(S)\bigr)dS,
+\tag{24}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(24)</span></div>
+<p>in degrees zero and one.</p>
+<p><strong>Flatness and exact rank.</strong> There is a direct sum of
+R-modules</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mspace width="0.222em"></mspace><mo>⊕</mo><mspace width="0.222em"></mspace><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{R[S]=\mathcal D_{u,t}(R[S])/dS\ \oplus\ R[S]_{&lt;q}.}
+\tag{25}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(25)</span></div>
+<p>In particular,</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>ℋ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>ℋ</mi><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℋ</mi><mo>≅</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{H^0(\mathcal C_{u,t})=0,\quad
+\mathcal H:=H^1(\mathcal C_{u,t})\cong R^q,
+\quad \mathcal H/(u,t)\mathcal H\cong E.}
+\tag{26}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(26)</span></div>
+<p><strong>Proof.</strong> If P has degree a and leading coefficient b,
+then
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uP'+(\chi-t)P</annotation></semantics></math>
+has degree a+q and leading coefficient b. It cannot be zero. For any
+polynomial F of degree at least q, subtract
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo>deg</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal D_{u,t}(bS^{\deg F-q})/dS</annotation></semantics></math>
+to remove exactly its highest term. Repeating terminates with a
+remainder of degree below q. Uniqueness follows from the leading-degree
+statement. The coefficients stay in R throughout. The same proof
+survives arbitrary base change of R, so (25) is a split exact
+presentation, not only a dimension count over its fraction field.
+Specializing u=t=0 turns (24) into multiplication by the literal
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math>,
+proving the final isomorphism.</p>
+<p>Let
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mo>red</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{red}_{u,t}</annotation></semantics></math>
+denote the resulting coefficient-linear remainder map and
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{&lt;q}</annotation></semantics></math>
+its specified polynomial section. The fibre map in (26) is
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>𝒟</mi></msub><mo>↦</mo><msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>χ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[P]_{\mathcal D}\mapsto[P|_{u=t=0}]_\chi</annotation></semantics></math>,
+with inverse induced by the chosen remainder basis.</p>
+<p>There is no ring quotient asserted for u nonzero. Its exact
+relationship to multiplication is the commutator</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+[\mathcal D_{u,t}/dS,M_S]=uI.
+\tag{27}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(27)</span></div>
+<p>For example,
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒟</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal D(1)/dS=\chi-t</annotation></semantics></math>,
+while
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒟</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal D(S)/dS=u+S(\chi-t)</annotation></semantics></math>;
+hence
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[S(\chi-t)]=-u[1]</annotation></semantics></math>
+in
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal H</annotation></semantics></math>.
+The image of the differential is generally not an ideal. Formula (25)
+supplies the coefficient module and its section; the next paragraph
+supplies the operator that really descends.</p>
+<h2 id="the-parameter-connection-contains-the-original-sum-operator">6.
+The parameter connection contains the original sum operator</h2>
+<p>On
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℋ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal H[u^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>,
+define</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\nabla_t[P]=[\partial_tP-S P/u].
+\tag{28}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(28)</span></div>
+<p>The exact relation</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+[\mathcal D_{u,t}/dS,\partial_t]=I,
+\qquad
+[\mathcal D_{u,t}/dS,-M_S/u]=-I
+\tag{29}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(29)</span></div>
+<p>proves that their sum commutes with the differential. Thus (28)
+descends without asserting that multiplication by S separately
+descends.</p>
+<p>In the specified remainder basis
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,S,\ldots,S^{q-1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
+the connection is</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>ℛ</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{\nabla_t=\partial_t-\frac{A(t)}u,
+\qquad A(t)=A+t\mathcal R,\quad
+\mathcal R=e_0e_{q-1}^{\,T}.}
+\tag{30}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(30)</span></div>
+<p>Here
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A e_a=e_{a+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
+for
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;q-1</annotation></semantics></math>
+and</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+A(t)e_{q-1}=(-c_0+t)e_0-c_1e_1-\cdots-c_{q-1}e_{q-1}.
+\tag{31}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(31)</span></div>
+<p>Only a degree-q polynomial has to be reduced in deriving (30);
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒟</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal D(1)=\chi-t</annotation></semantics></math>
+gives exactly (31), including the sign of t.</p>
+<p>The operator
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-u\nabla_t=-u\partial_t+A(t)</annotation></semantics></math>
+is regular in u and obeys the exact coefficient rule
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>v</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-u\nabla_t)(fv)=f(-u\nabla_t)v-u(\partial_t f)v</annotation></semantics></math>.
+It is a parameter-scaled connection in the t direction, not a derivative
+in u. Its reduction at u=0 is the honest endomorphism
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(t)</annotation></semantics></math>,
+and</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mspace width="0.222em"></mspace><mo>mod</mo><mspace width="0.222em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mspace width="0.333em"></mspace><mtext mathvariant="normal"> on </mtext><mspace width="0.333em"></mspace></mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{(-u\nabla_t)\bmod(u,t)=A=M_S\text{ on }E.}
+\tag{32}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(32)</span></div>
+<p>This is the precise operator bridge. It contains a minus sign fixed
+by (24) and (28). The original arithmetic scaling is not renamed
+Frobenius.</p>
+<h3 id="carry-the-parameter-through-the-original-theta-representative">Carry
+the parameter through the original theta representative</h3>
+<p>Let
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒱</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mover><mo>⊗</mo><mo accent="true">̂</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_N=\mathcal V R_N:E\to\mathscr B^{\widehat\otimes k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
+On its specified test-function domain, use the earlier full jet map
+followed by the fixed equivariant cyclic retraction to obtain
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_E</annotation></semantics></math>
+with</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mrow><mspace width="0.333em"></mspace><mtext mathvariant="normal"> kills the original theta boundaries</mtext></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+j_Er_N=I_E,\quad j_ED^{(k)}=Aj_E,
+\quad j_E\text{ kills the original theta boundaries}.
+\tag{33}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(33)</span></div>
+<p>The construction of
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_E</annotation></semantics></math>
+uses the retained equivariant retraction, not an arbitrary quotient of
+the full tensor packet. Define the concrete finite-rank perturbation</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>ℛ</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{D_t^{(k)}=D^{(k)}+t\,r_N\mathcal R j_E.}
+\tag{34}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(34)</span></div>
+<p>Then</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+j_ED_t^{(k)}=A(t)j_E,
+\qquad
+\boxed{D_t^{(k)}r_N-r_NA(t)=D^{(k)}r_N-r_NA=dK_N.}
+\tag{35}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(35)</span></div>
+<p>Both identities follow by substitution. The added term vanishes on
+every original boundary because
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_E</annotation></semantics></math>
+does. Thus the deformation has an explicit source realization preserving
+the original relation primitive. At t=0 it is exactly the original
+generator. At t nonzero it is the displayed finite-rank deformation, not
+a new assertion about the zero set of
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+<p>The canonical arithmetic metric remains
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_N</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Its parameter control is explicitly</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo><mover><mi>t</mi><mo accent="true">‾</mo></mover><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><msup><mi>ℛ</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{W_N(t)=W_N(0)+tG_N\mathcal R+\bar t\,\mathcal R^*G_N.}
+\tag{36}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36)</span></div>
+<p>No positive metric has been selected from the exponential family to
+replace
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+<h3 id="the-exponential-periods-are-constructed-and-proved-to-span">6.1
+The exponential periods are constructed and proved to span</h3>
+<p>Fix a nonzero complex u, write d=q+1, and fix an argument of u.
+Set</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>arg</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\theta_0=\frac{\arg u+\pi}{d},\qquad
+\theta_j=\theta_0+\frac{2\pi j}{d}\quad(0\le j&lt;d).
+\tag{36a}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36a)</span></div>
+<p>Let
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mo>ℓ</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_j</annotation></semantics></math>
+be the oriented ray from zero to infinity with angle
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_j</annotation></semantics></math>,
+and set
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><msub><mo>ℓ</mo><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mo>ℓ</mo><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_j=-\ell_0+\ell_j</annotation></semantics></math>
+for
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\le j\le q</annotation></semantics></math>.
+The minus sign makes the initial endpoint contributions cancel. For a
+polynomial P define</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow></msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>Π</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\mathcal P_j(P)=\int_{\Gamma_j}e^{\Phi_t(S)/u}P(S)\,dS,
+\qquad
+\Pi_{jb}(u,t)=\mathcal P_j(S^b),\quad 0\le b&lt;q.
+\tag{36b}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36b)</span></div>
+<p>These are absolutely convergent contour integrals. Along each ray the
+leading exponent is
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-r^d/(d|u|)</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Lower-degree terms are dominated by that negative term outside a
+sufficiently large compact interval, uniformly when the lower
+coefficients and t range over a compact set. The same estimate after
+multiplying by any polynomial proves holomorphic dependence on those
+parameters and justifies their differentiation under the integral.</p>
+<p>Integration of the actual total derivative gives</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form="prefix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mi>d</mi><mo form="prefix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow></msup><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\mathcal P_j\bigl(uP'+(\chi-t)P\bigr)
+=u\int_{\Gamma_j}d\bigl(e^{\Phi_t/u}P\bigr)=0.
+\tag{36c}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36c)</span></div>
+<p>Both ends at infinity have zero boundary value, and the two values at
+zero cancel. Consequently the periods descend through the very
+differential (24), not through a quotient chosen afterwards.</p>
+<p><strong>The period matrix is invertible for every choice of the lower
+coefficients and every t, at this fixed nonzero u.</strong> Here is a
+direct proof, including the singular critical-point cases.</p>
+<p>Temporarily regard the coefficients
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_0,\ldots,c_{q-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
+as independent parameters. The coefficient-direction connections are</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>∂</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\nabla_{c_a}=\partial_{c_a}+\frac{S^{a+1}}{(a+1)u},
+\qquad [\mathcal D/dS,\nabla_{c_a}]=0.
+\tag{36d}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36d)</span></div>
+<p>The two commutator terms are
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-S^a</annotation></semantics></math>
+and
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>+</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+S^a</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Reducing
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{a+b+1}/((a+1)u)</annotation></semantics></math>
+by (25) gives the b-th column of a q-by-q matrix
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_a</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Every entry is a polynomial in the lower coefficients and t, with powers
+of the fixed
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Thus</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mi>Π</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Π</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>Π</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>Π</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\partial_{c_a}\Pi=\Pi B_a,\qquad
+\partial_t\Pi=-\Pi A(t)/u.
+\tag{36e}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36e)</span></div>
+<p>At the monomial potential
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=S^d/d</annotation></semantics></math>,
+t=0, direct substitution in the ray integral gives</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Π</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mi>Γ</mi><mspace width="-0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\Pi_{jb}^{\mathrm{mon}}
+=\frac{(d|u|)^{(b+1)/d}}{d}
+\Gamma\!\left(\frac{b+1}{d}\right)
+ e^{i(b+1)\theta_0}
+ \left(e^{2\pi i j(b+1)/d}-1\right).
+\tag{36f}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36f)</span></div>
+<p>All Gamma factors, all powers, and all phases remain. The final
+q-by-q matrix is nonsingular: a dependence among its columns would give
+a polynomial
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{l=1}^{q}a_l(z^l-1)</annotation></semantics></math>
+of degree at most q vanishing at every d-th root of unity, including 1.
+It has d=q+1 distinct zeros, so it is the zero polynomial and every
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_l</annotation></semantics></math>
+vanishes.</p>
+<p>For the phase matrix
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{jl}=e^{2\pi i jl/d}-1</annotation></semantics></math>,
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\le j,l&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>,
+finite root-of-unity summation also gives
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><msup><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>T</mi></msup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^*V=d(I_q+\mathbf1\mathbf1^T)</annotation></semantics></math>
+and
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>det</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mi>V</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(V^*V)=d^d</annotation></semantics></math>.
+This evaluates its nonzero size without absorbing any of the Gamma
+factors in (36f).</p>
+<p>Join this monomial parameter to the actual parameter by a straight
+line in the coefficient space, keeping the rays fixed. Equations (36e)
+give
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Π</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Π</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi'=\Pi B</annotation></semantics></math>
+with an everywhere continuous, explicitly reduced matrix B.
+Differentiation of the determinant, valid through the adjugate formula
+even at a putative singular matrix, yields</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>det</mo><mi>Π</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>Tr</mo><mi>B</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>det</mo><mi>Π</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+(\det\Pi)'=(\operatorname{Tr}B)\det\Pi.
+\tag{36g}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36g)</span></div>
+<p>Its initial value is nonzero by (36f). The scalar equation has
+solution given by that value times the exponential of the integral of
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>Tr</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tr}B</annotation></semantics></math>,
+and never vanishes. This proves the stated invertibility without
+assuming simple critical points or a basis of unproved cycles.</p>
+<p>It follows that the actual period map is the coefficient-linear
+isomorphism</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mspace width="0.222em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.222em"></mspace></mrow></mover><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>↦</mo><mi>Π</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>red</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{\mathcal H_{u,t}\xrightarrow{\ (\mathcal P_1,\ldots,\mathcal P_q)\ }\mathbb C^q,
+\qquad [P]\longmapsto\Pi(u,t)\operatorname{red}_{u,t}(P),}
+\tag{36h}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36h)</span></div>
+<p>with inverse given by
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Π</mi><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi(u,t)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
+followed by the specified degree-below-q section. Polynomial-exponential
+de Rham cohomology and rapid-decay period pairings are established
+machinery; (36a)–(36h) give an elementary proof of the particular
+one-variable comparison used here, with its contours and factors
+fixed.</p>
+<h3 id="transport-the-period-norm-through-the-original-source-map">6.2
+Transport the period norm through the original source map</h3>
+<p>Use the explicit coefficient identification</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>ȷ</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>E</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mo accent="false">∼</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo>↦</mo><msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><msub><mo>rem</mo><mi>χ</mi></msub><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\jmath_{u,t}:E\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal H_{u,t},
+\qquad[P]_\chi\longmapsto[\operatorname{rem}_\chi P]_{\mathcal D_{u,t}}.
+\tag{36i}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36i)</span></div>
+<p>This is a complex-linear basis comparison. It is not a ring morphism
+when the target differential image is not an ideal. Equations (30)–(35)
+are the operator comparisons attached to it.</p>
+<p>The original representative image now has an actual period
+isomorphism and inverse:</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mspace width="0.222em"></mspace><mi>Π</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mspace width="0.222em"></mspace></mrow></mover><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>y</mi><mo>↦</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>Π</mi><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{r_N(E)\xrightarrow{\ \Pi(u,t)j_E\ }\mathbb C^q,
+\qquad y\longmapsto r_N\Pi(u,t)^{-1}y.}
+\tag{36j}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36j)</span></div>
+<p>Their two composites are identities on the specified domains, by
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_Er_N=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
+The same map applied to a retained theta boundary gives zero because
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_E</annotation></semantics></math>
+kills that boundary; its split lift gives the receiving supported zero,
+not external absence.</p>
+<p>The operator comparison also holds at the level of actual period
+functions. For a t-independent F in the admitted theta test-function
+domain, (33), (35), and (36e) give</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>u</mi><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo form="prefix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Π</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mi>F</mi><mo form="postfix" maxsize="1.2" minsize="1.2" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Π</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{-u\,\partial_t\bigl(\Pi(u,t)j_EF\bigr)
+=\Pi(u,t)j_ED_t^{(k)}F.}
+\tag{36j.1}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36j.1)</span></div>
+<p>At t=0 the right side uses the original
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
+On the full test-function domain the kernel of this period observation
+is exactly
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>ker</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker j_E</annotation></semantics></math>,
+because
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>
+is invertible. It remains an explicit retained kernel; the isomorphism
+(36j) concerns only the stated representative image
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_N(E)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+<p>Give the period-coordinate target its explicitly chosen ordinary
+coordinate Gram
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_q</annotation></semantics></math>
+and write</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Π</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Π</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mi>Π</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Π</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+H_\Pi=\Pi^*\Pi,\qquad
+B_N^{\mathrm{per}}=G_N^{-1}H_\Pi.
+\tag{36k}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36k)</span></div>
+<p>Then
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_N^{\mathrm{per}}</annotation></semantics></math>
+is positive self-adjoint in the original
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_N</annotation></semantics></math>,
+and</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="false">∥</mo><mi>Π</mi><mi>v</mi><msup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="false">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mo>log</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>log</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>det</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo>det</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\|\Pi v\|^2=v^*G_NB_N^{\mathrm{per}}v,
+\qquad
+\boxed{\log\frac{V_i}{V_j}
+=\log\frac{\det B_j^{\mathrm{per}}}{\det B_i^{\mathrm{per}}}.}
+\tag{36l}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36l)</span></div>
+<p>The latter follows from
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>det</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Π</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det B_N^{\mathrm{per}}=\det H_\Pi/\det G_N</annotation></semantics></math>
+at the same (u,t). Thus the endpoint cost has been carried into two
+explicit period-to-source norm comparisons; it has not been set to zero
+by choosing period coordinates. The degree of the cyclic packet and the
+fixed contour-coordinate metric remain in the formula. Equation (36l) is
+a comparison identity, not a uniform bound on those determinants.</p>
+<p>For q=1,
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(S)=S-\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>,
+real u&gt;0, and the contour
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\mathbb R</annotation></semantics></math>
+oriented from
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-i\infty</annotation></semantics></math>
+to
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>+</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+i\infty</annotation></semantics></math>,
+the integral is exactly</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>Π</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow></msqrt><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mo>exp</mo><mspace width="-0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>log</mo><mi>Π</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{\Pi(u,t)=i\sqrt{2\pi u}\,
+\exp\!\left(-\frac{(\lambda+t)^2}{2u}\right),
+\qquad -u\partial_t\log\Pi=\lambda+t.}
+\tag{36m}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(36m)</span></div>
+<p>This uses the displayed orientation; it is the negative of the q=1
+ray-difference cycle in (36a). The positive metric value for real
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda,t</annotation></semantics></math>
+is
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>exp</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>/</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi u\exp(- (\lambda+t)^2/u)</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Its exponentially singular u-behaviour is retained. Thus even in rank
+one, passage through u=0 is a substantive norm-comparison question, not
+a consequence of finite-field purity of a different operator.</p>
+<h2 id="a-constant-residue-pairing-gives-the-exact-dual-connection">7. A
+constant residue pairing gives the exact dual connection</h2>
+<p>On the remainder basis define the complex-bilinear form</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mo>Res</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\mathscr S_t(P,Q)=\sum_{\chi(\lambda)=t}
+\operatorname{Res}_{S=\lambda}\frac{P(S)Q(S)}{\chi(S)-t}\,dS.
+\tag{37}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(37)</span></div>
+<p>Multiplicity is handled by the residue, not by an assumption that the
+roots are simple. The form can equivalently be evaluated as the
+coefficient of
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
+in the Laurent expansion at infinity of the displayed rational function.
+This is minus the residue at infinity, so the sign agrees with the sum
+of the finite residues.</p>
+<p>For degree-below-q P,Q,</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>𝒮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒮</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{\mathscr S_t(P,Q)=\mathscr S_0(P,Q).}
+\tag{38}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(38)</span></div>
+<p>Indeed,</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>χ</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\frac1{\chi-t}-\frac1\chi=\frac{t}{\chi(\chi-t)},
+</annotation></semantics></math></div>
+<p>and multiplication by PQ, whose degree is at most
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2q-2</annotation></semantics></math>,
+gives a Laurent expansion beginning at
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Its
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
+coefficient is zero. Let
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>𝖲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf S</annotation></semantics></math>
+be the constant matrix. Its entries below the first nonzero antidiagonal
+vanish and that antidiagonal has entries one, so</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>det</mo><mi>𝖲</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>𝖲</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\det\mathsf S=(-1)^{q(q-1)/2},\qquad
+\boxed{A(t)^T\mathsf S=\mathsf S A(t).}
+\tag{39}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(39)</span></div>
+<p>The latter equation follows because multiplication by S is
+self-adjoint for the residue pairing on the algebra
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb C[S]/(\chi-t)</annotation></semantics></math>;
+(30) is precisely its multiplication matrix in this basis.</p>
+<p>Let
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_u</annotation></semantics></math>
+be the base involution
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>↦</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,t)\mapsto(-u,t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
+The actual dual map is</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>ℋ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><mi>ℋ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>v</mi><mo>↦</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>↦</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>w</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{\mathcal H[u^{-1}]\longrightarrow
+(\iota_u^*\mathcal H[u^{-1}])^\vee,
+\qquad v\longmapsto(w\mapsto v^T\mathsf S w).}
+\tag{40}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(40)</span></div>
+<p>It is horizontal:</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>w</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msubsup><mi>∇</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mi>v</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msubsup><mi>∇</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>w</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\partial_t(v^T\mathsf S w)
+=(\nabla_t^{u}v)^T\mathsf S w
++v^T\mathsf S(\nabla_t^{-u}w).
+\tag{41}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(41)</span></div>
+<p>This is a bilinear duality; the original Hermitian arithmetic pairing
+is connected on the special fibre by its retained antilinear reflection
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>↦</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mo>†</mo><mi>k</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mapsto f^{\dagger_k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
+In particular the Jacobian contraction is still</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>𝒮</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mo>†</mo><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>χ</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>v</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>Tr</mo><mi>E</mi></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mo>†</mo><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{\mathscr S_0(f^{\dagger_k},\chi'v)
+=\operatorname{Tr}_{E}M_{f^{\dagger_k}v}.}
+\tag{42}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(42)</span></div>
+<p>The proof is the local equality
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>χ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>ℓ</mo><mi>λ</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi'/\chi=\ell_\lambda/(S-\lambda)+</annotation></semantics></math>
+a holomorphic term. The trace on the full original tensor or symmetric
+packet is still obtained through
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>
+and its retained complement; (42) is not silently substituted for that
+larger trace. Likewise the original factor
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msubsup><mi>υ</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>⊗</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon_h^{\otimes k}</annotation></semantics></math>
+stays in (5) and (6).</p>
+<h2 id="the-split-coefficient-maps-and-the-original-derivative-tower">8.
+The split coefficient maps and the original derivative tower</h2>
+<p>The family is over
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathbb C[u,t]</annotation></semantics></math>,
+with scalar map
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathbb C)\to G(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Its reconstruction at any original support label
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mo>ℓ</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>
+has classes
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>ℓ</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell,v)</annotation></semantics></math>,
+and</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>ℓ</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>ℓ</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>ℓ</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+e_R(\ell,v)=(\ell,0),\qquad \tau(\ell,v)=\tau.
+\tag{43}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(43)</span></div>
+<p>The specialization
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to\mathbb C</annotation></semantics></math>,
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>↦</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,t\mapsto0</annotation></semantics></math>,
+lifts to a semiring map
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(R)\to G(\mathbb C)</annotation></semantics></math>,
+fixing
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>
+and carrying both
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>•</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>•</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^\bullet,t^\bullet</annotation></semantics></math>
+to the receiving e. The induced coefficient map is</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>ℓ</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>𝒟</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>↦</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mo>ℓ</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+(\ell,[P]_{\mathcal D})\longmapsto
+(\ell,[P(0,0,S)]_\chi).
+\tag{44}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(44)</span></div>
+<p>For every P, the exact family relation is</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>χ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>′</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant="normal">in </mtext><mspace width="0.333em"></mspace></mrow><mi>ℋ</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+[\chi P]=t[P]-u[P']\quad\text{in }\mathcal H.
+\tag{45}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(45)</span></div>
+<p>At u=t=0, this becomes the original supported zero. This family
+identity must not be substituted for the already formalized derivative
+between powers of the original relation ideal. Their precise polynomial
+relationship is</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>χ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>χ</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>χ</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>χ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>χ</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>P</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\partial_S(\chi P)=\chi'P+\chi P',\quad
+[\partial_S(\chi P)]_\chi=[\chi'P]_\chi,
+\tag{46}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(46)</span></div>
+<p>whereas the u-term of (45) contains
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-P'</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Both are obtained from the same polynomial and the displayed maps. The
+conormal response
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi'P</annotation></semantics></math>
+remains exactly the Jacobian in (42). None of the new parameters is
+another scalar zero-adjunction, and no original ideal-power level is
+replaced by a power of a contracted first ideal.</p>
+<h2 id="this-potential-has-actual-deligne-exponential-sheaf-fibres">9.
+This potential has actual Deligne exponential-sheaf fibres</h2>
+<p>Let
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_0</annotation></semantics></math>
+be the finitely generated subring of
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb C</annotation></semantics></math>
+generated by the coefficients
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_a</annotation></semantics></math>
+and
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>!</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(q+1)!</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Keep its specified embedding into
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb C</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Equations (23) and (24) are defined over
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_0[u,t]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+<p>For every specified ring map
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_0\to\mathbb F_Q</annotation></semantics></math>
+with residue characteristic
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;q+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
+and every
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_0\in\mathbb F_Q^*</annotation></semantics></math>
+and
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_0\in\mathbb F_Q</annotation></semantics></math>,
+the same potential gives the explicit Artin–Schreier cover</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>S</mi></mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{Y^p-Y=\frac{\Phi(S)-t_0S}{u_0}.}
+\tag{47}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(47)</span></div>
+<p>The infinity calculation in Deligne’s lissity proof can be made
+explicitly on this family. Put
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=q+1</annotation></semantics></math>
+and
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=1/S</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Then</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\frac{\Phi(S)-tS}{u}=w^{-d}b(w,u,t),\qquad
+b(w,u,t)=\frac1u\left[\frac1d+\sum_{a=0}^{q-1}\frac{c_a}{a+1}w^{q-a}-t w^q\right].
+\tag{47a}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(47a)</span></div>
+<p>At w=0 its value is the unit
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(du)</annotation></semantics></math>.
+On the neighborhood where b is invertible, adjoin the unit v by
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b v^d=1</annotation></semantics></math>
+and define
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_\infty=w v</annotation></semantics></math>.
+This is an explicit finite étale cover: the derivative in v is
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d b v^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
+whose inverse on the cover is v/d. The new relative coordinate has
+invertible derivative at w=0. Direct substitution gives</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>∞</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{\frac{\Phi(S)-tS}{u}=x_\infty^{-d},\qquad
+x_\infty=w v,\quad b v^d=1.}
+\tag{47b}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(47b)</span></div>
+<p>Every coefficient in b is retained, as is the cover’s root-of-unity
+action
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\mapsto\zeta v</annotation></semantics></math>,
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta^d=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
+This is the local, étale source map used to see a constant exponential
+model at infinity. Together with the locally constant sheaf on the
+smooth affine part, it is exactly the local-acyclicity mechanism used in
+Deligne’s §3.7.3. The open u nonzero remains necessary; this map makes
+no claim at u=0.</p>
+<p>Choose a nontrivial additive character
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>ℚ</mi><mo accent="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>ℓ</mo><mo>*</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:\mathbb F_p\to\overline{\mathbb Q}_\ell^*</annotation></semantics></math>,
+with
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>ℓ</mo><mo>≠</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\ne p</annotation></semantics></math>,
+and let
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>Φ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal L_\psi((\Phi-t_0S)/u_0)</annotation></semantics></math>
+be the corresponding eigensheaf. The polynomial on the right has degree
+q+1, prime to p, and leading coefficient
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/((q+1)u_0)</annotation></semantics></math>.
+In one variable its leading projective zero locus in
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb P^0</annotation></semantics></math>
+is empty, and is smooth. Thus the precise hypotheses of Deligne §3.7.2
+hold.</p>
+<p>His theorem supplies</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><msub><mover><mi>𝔽</mi><mo accent="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>Q</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>Φ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width="0.222em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mo>dim</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mspace width="0.333em"></mspace><mtext mathvariant="normal"> is pure of weight </mtext><mspace width="0.333em"></mspace></mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\boxed{
+H_c^a(\mathbb A^1_{\overline{\mathbb F}_Q},
+\mathcal L_\psi((\Phi-t_0S)/u_0))=0\ (a\ne1),\quad
+\dim H_c^1=q,\quad H_c^1\text{ is pure of weight }1.
+}
+\tag{48}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(48)</span></div>
+<p>The universal-polynomial argument in §§3.7.3–3.7.4 supplies a lisse
+family on the parameter open
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\ne0</annotation></semantics></math>,
+after that finite-field specialization. This is a direct application of
+Deligne’s result, not a new proof of it.</p>
+<p>The exact realization diagram is a span of specified base
+changes:</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>Φ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>!</mi></msub><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+(R_0,\Phi,u,t)\longrightarrow
+\begin{cases}
+(\mathbb C,\mathcal C_{u,t},\nabla_t),\\
+(\mathbb F_Q,\mathcal L_\psi((\Phi-tS)/u),R^1p_!).
+\end{cases}
+\tag{49}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(49)</span></div>
+<p>Its characteristic-zero branch has the proved specialization (26) and
+operator formula (32). Its finite-field branch has Frobenius and (48).
+No field map
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb C\to\mathbb F_Q</annotation></semantics></math>
+is asserted; both arrows start at the actual ring
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_0</annotation></semantics></math>.
+Nor is a comparison equating the two operators supplied. The arithmetic
+theta metric
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_N</annotation></semantics></math>
+is not part of Deligne’s finite-field conclusion until an additional
+comparison theorem carries it there.</p>
+<p>This places the cyclic relation in a genuine family of the kind
+Deligne studies, instead of attributing his nonconstant-constituent
+argument to the constant representative curve. It leaves an explicit
+operator/norm comparison to investigate.</p>
+<h3 id="a-calibration-of-the-operator-distinction">A calibration of the
+operator distinction</h3>
+<p>Take the declared algebraic polynomial
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(S)=S-3/4</annotation></semantics></math>;
+this is not an asserted zeta packet. Then</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>S</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\Phi(S)=S^2/2-3S/4,\qquad A(t)=3/4+t,
+\qquad\nabla_t=\partial_t-(3/4+t)/u.
+\tag{50}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(50)</span></div>
+<p>All the rank, specialization, and duality results above hold. At
+every finite-field realization of characteristic greater than two where
+the coefficient is defined, (48) still gives weight-one Frobenius. The
+characteristic-zero special coefficient remains
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/4</annotation></semantics></math>.
+This exact example shows why a purity theorem for the displayed
+Artin–Schreier family does not, by itself, locate the spectrum of (32).
+The additional comparison must use the specific original arithmetic
+source, not only the fact that a polynomial-exponential realization
+exists.</p>
+<h2 id="what-this-changes-in-the-programme">10. What this changes in the
+programme</h2>
+<p>The predecessor’s nonconcentration problem remains</p>
+<div class="equation-block"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mo>ℓ</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></munderover><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><msup><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mo>ℓ</mo></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\mathcal B_{h,k}
+=\sum_{\ell=0}^1\int_{|z|&lt;1}\log(1/|z|^2)\,d\mu_\ell.
+\tag{51}
+</annotation></semantics></math><span class="equation-number">(51)</span></div>
+<p>The reviewed source-norm theorem and consecutive-window result still
+force the inherited off-line threshold of four. Neither log extension,
+finite ramification, nor saturation changes its original endpoint metric
+cost; (20) and (21) state precisely how those operations act.</p>
+<p>What is added is a cohomological family with the exact original
+cyclic special fibre, an explicit connection containing A, an explicit
+dual connection, and finite-field exponential fibres covered by a
+theorem actually read in Deligne. The source-side realization (34)–(36)
+specifies the finite-rank deformation and keeps the theta boundary. The
+contour periods now give actual isomorphisms at u nonzero and the
+matrices
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>Π</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mi>Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_N^{\mathrm{per}}=G_N^{-1}\Pi^*\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>
+give their exact comparison to the fixed arithmetic metric. Equation
+(36l) carries the endpoint volume into their determinant ratio. The
+remaining estimate must control those specified matrices through
+singular u=0 and along the original tensor/degree windows. The existence
+of the period isomorphism or of scalar support is not itself that
+uniform estimate.</p>
+<p>This continuation proves no new upper bound contradicting the
+endpoint threshold. It does provide the actual family and the exact maps
+on which a Deligne-style comparison can be attempted, after calculating
+and retaining the limitations of the earlier constant curve.</p>
+<h2 id="references-and-verification-scope">References and verification
+scope</h2>
+<p>Deligne, P. (1980), <em>La conjecture de Weil II</em>, Publications
+Mathématiques de l’IHÉS 52, 137–252. Selected current source records:
+printed pages 201–203, 206, 215–216, 248. Retained French and English
+TeX windows are listed in <code>SOURCE_REVIEW.md</code>.</p>
+<p>The Stacks Project, Section 50.15, Tag 0FMU, <em>Log poles along a
+divisor</em>. Its residue exact sequence fixes the complexes in (15);
+the actual calculations in (12), (13), and (18) are given here. The
+comparison is made in complexes of complex vector-space sheaves, since
+their connections are coefficient-linear, not
+<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal O_X</annotation></semantics></math>-linear.</p>
+<p>The new exact tests are finite regression checks of the displayed
+polynomial, matrix, quotient, connection, residue, and Cech formulas.
+They do not certify infinite arithmetic integrals, compile new Lean
+modules, or prove a realization comparison absent from this note. The
+frozen predecessor is preserved. No remote branch is modified by this
+delivery.</p>
+<p>Marco Hien (2009), <em>Periods for flat algebraic connections</em>,
+Inventiones Mathematicae 178, 1–22, DOI 10.1007/s00222-009-0185-7, is
+primary background for de Rham/rapid-decay duality. Only its
+bibliographic record and abstract were consulted here; no general
+theorem from it is needed in the direct contour proof (36a)–(36h).</p>
+</body>
+</html>
